# Intro
One 29.7 Msun star in a uniform, periodic box of cold (50 K), zero-metallicity
molecular gas, with the ISRF transport on (`ISRF_propagation: 1`) and the H2 line
absorption of the transport on (`ISRF_H2_line_absorption: 1`). The star's
Lyman-Werner (LW) light is absorbed by the H2 lines along its path. The check compares,
for every gas particle, the fraction of its own LW energy that the lines let through
(`LWOwnDissociatingSpecificEnergies` over `LWOwnSpecificEnergies`) with the shielding
function at the column `N_H2 = n_H2 r`,
where `n_H2` is the particle's own H2 density and `r` its distance to the star. The
shielding function is the Wolcott-Green and Haiman (2019) fit that geajay uses.

The run is cooling with geajay only: with `ISRF_H2_line_absorption: 1` SWIFT tells the
library not to shield the transported LW rate a second time, so no cooling parameter is
needed for it. Official Grackle refuses `ISRF_H2_line_absorption: 1`.

## What the run holds fixed
The check reads the end of a short run in which H2 and the temperature stay frozen. The
parameters therefore switch off photoionization, stellar winds and supernovae, and set the
light speed to `unlimited`. The light crosses one hop per step, so it needs several steps to
reach the whole box. The steps are short so that the H2 stays frozen while the light
arrives; the run stops after 8 steps and the check reads the last snapshot. The check
refuses a run that departs from these settings. Only particles at `r < L/2 - 2h` are read,
so that the nearest periodic image of the star is the right one.

# Configure
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=geajay_3 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-geajay=path/to/geajay --with-chealpix=path/to/chealpix
make -j
```
The geajay build must provide the option that leaves the transported LW rate out of its H2
shielding; without it SWIFT stops at start-up. The packet
transport needs a radiation table whose `Data/Radiation` group carries
`MeanPhotonEnergyPE` and `Integrated_MeanPhotonEnergyPE`; see the README of
`ISRFH2Photodissociation` for how to obtain it.

# Run
```
./run.sh                      # main leg, then the check
leg=switch_off ./run.sh       # line absorption off: the check must stop on the missing line fields
leg=stale_key ./run.sh        # an old GeajayCooling:h2_transported_lw_rate_shielding on the command line has no effect
ranks=2 run_name=two_ranks ./run.sh
leg=restart ./run.sh          # light speed 0.99 c (params_restart.yml), stops after step 17
```
`level`, `gas_density`, `gas_mass`, `h2_ratio` (initial `nH2/nH`) and `n_threads`
override the defaults. The default `h2_ratio` of 3e-4 gives columns from 1e17 to
about 2e18 cm^-2 over the box.

# Checking
`checkH2Attenuation.py` reads `used_parameters.yml`, `fixture.json` (written by
`makeIC.py`), the first and last snapshot, the SWIFT log (`--startup-log output.log`) and
`timesteps.txt` (`--timesteps`). SWIFT does not write the LW shielding option to
`used_parameters.yml`, so the check reads it from the start-up line
`h2_transported_lw_rate_shielding = 1` that SWIFT prints. `run.sh` passes the log.

The parameter files set `GEARFeedback:ISRF_LW_population_dissociation: 0`, so the
dissociating light of the star is its LW light times the line transmission. With 1 and a
table that carries `L_LW_dissociating`, it is also multiplied by the dissociation factor
`f` of the star (`L_LW_dissociating / L_LW` from the table); pass that value to the
`starlit` and `velocity` checks with `--population-factor f`. SWIFT caps the kernel mean of
the dissociating energy at the largest factor of the table times the LW energy; `starlit`
reads that bound from the line `ISRF LW dissociation factor bound` of the start-up log.

`python3 checkH2Attenuation.py starlit --first snap/snapshot_0001.hdf5 --last snap/snapshot_0009.hdf5 --startup-log output.log --timesteps timesteps.txt`

The reference is `snapshot_0001`, the first snapshot written after the first step and
before any probe is lit. The check compares, for every probe, the transmission of the
own LW light with the shielding function at the column `n_H2 r`, the kernel mean of that
transmission and of the LW energies that the cooling reads, the number and extent of the lit
probes, and the carried H2 column.

The exit class of the check is 0 for a pass, 1 for a failed bar, 2 for a missing file,
field or key, 3 for a non-finite value and 4 for a void run (a premise or a positive
control is not met). A void run never passes. `parity` alone also returns 5 for a pass
without a counters file.

Two runs are compared particle by particle with `parity`, on `LWSpecificEnergies`,
`LWDissociatingSpecificEnergies` and `LWLineH2ColumnDensities`:
```
python3 checkH2Attenuation.py parity --mode restart --a ref/snap/snapshot_0032.hdf5 --b resumed/snap/snapshot_0032.hdf5 --stop ref/snap/snapshot_0017.hdf5
python3 checkH2Attenuation.py parity --mode ranks --a one/snap/snapshot_0009.hdf5 --b two/snap/snapshot_0009.hdf5 --previous one/snap/snapshot_0008.hdf5 --first one/snap/snapshot_0001.hdf5 --params one/used_parameters.yml
```
- `restart`: every `LW` and `Packet` field must be equal bit for bit (same binary, same
  rank count, one thread).
- `ranks`: the float sums run in another order, so the fields agree within a round-off bar.

`testCheckH2Attenuation.py WORK_DIR` runs the check on synthetic snapshots, with a planted
error in each case, and prints the exit class of each.
